 "probability": 0.08, "rationale": "A sufficiently large abrupt loss increase can suppress both traffic and OSC and therefore resemble a cut. Complete, simultaneous bidirectional LOS across two S16 cables is more characteristic of physical severing than a bend, splice degradation, or crushed cable.", "grounded": ["oms.S16-S03.span0"], "n_grounded": 1, "note": ""}, {"fault_mode": "CONNECTOR_PULLED", "location_kind": "term", "term": "oms_input", "anchor_symptom": "O1", "anchor_service": null, "element": null, "probability": 0.04, "rationale": "Recent maintenance at S16 makes a disconnected section-input patch plausible and could explain loss toward S03. It is strongly disfavoured because one outbound patch does not explain reverse-direction OSC loss or the simultaneous S16\u2013S07 failure, and the S16\u2013S03 booster telemetry reportedly did not change.", "grounded": ["oms.S16-S03"], "n_grounded": 1, "note": ""}, {"fault_mode": "WSS_PORT_FAIL", "location_kind": "shared", "term": null, "anchor_symptom": null, "anchor_service": null, "element": "E1", "probability": 0.02, "rationale": "The S16 ROADM is the stated common dependency of all affected working paths, so a WSS input-port failure could create a broad service impact. However, a WSS fault should not lose the OSC on external spans and cannot readily explain bidirectional LOS on two degrees.", "grounded": ["roadm.S16"], "n_grounded": 1, "note": ""}], "probability_other": 0.04, "sum_stated": 1.0}, "llm": {"provider": "openai", "model": "gpt-5.6"}, "llm_calls": [{"role": "proposer", "latency_s": 30.899983167648315, "input_tokens": 16411, "output_tokens": 2545, "reasoning_tokens": 2004, "ok": true, "error": ""}]}